                <pb facs="0355" n="348" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg348"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/9227: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hankow</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Josselyn</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-07T10:00:00+08:00">August 7, 1937—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 7—1 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>On inquiry this morning I have been informed by the Mayor of Hankow that
                    yesterday Acting Japanese Consul General called on him and stated that he had
                    received orders from Tokyo to withdraw all of his nationals from Hankow
                    including Navy and naval forces ashore. Three naval vessels escorting Japanese
                    merchant vessels will leave today and the remaining two tomorrow. Acting Consul
                    General and staff will remain for the time being. Mayor and Acting Japanese
                    Consul General are meeting this morning to arrange details. Sent to Nanking,
                    repeated to Peiping, Shanghai. Tokyo informed by telegraph.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Josselyn</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
